                0.383
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_table_6.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td>Experiences</td><td>Observations</td><td>Questions</td></tr><tr><td>When you get a smallcut on your skin</td><td>Red blood oozes out from the cut.A clot is formed after some time.</td><td>What causesblood to clot?</td></tr><tr><td>When you get a skininfection</td><td>The area turns red and perhapsslightly swollen. You may have a fever.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>When you exerciseor run</td><td>You breathe faster. Your face mayturn red.</td><td></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.393,
            0.888,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The everyday experiences mentioned above are related to blood and its components."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.43,
            0.889,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. The red colour of blood is due to haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein in the Red Blood Cells (RBCs). RBCs live for about 4 months and are replaced regularly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.484,
            0.758,
            0.504
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Platelets help in blood clotting at the site of the injury."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.506,
            0.89,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. During exercise or running, muscles need more oxygen, so breathing becomes faster and blood flow increases (face appears red)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.542,
            0.89,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. White Blood Cells (WBCs) collect at infected areas, causing pus formation and inflammation (causing redness and swelling, and possible pus formation at the infected area)."
            }
          ]
        },
